mdx","sourceFileName":"routing.mdx","sourceFileDir":"storely/guides","contentType":"mdx","flattenedPath":"storely/guides/routing"},"type":"Doc","slug":"/storely/guides/routing","slugAsParams":"storely/guides/routing","instance":"storely"},{"title":"RTL Support","description":"Implement and use Right-to-Left (RTL) text direction in Storely","published":true,"featured":false,"component":false,"toc":true,"body":{"raw":"\\n# RTL Support\\n\\nStorely includes built-in support for Right-to-Left (RTL) languages like Arabic and Hebrew. This guide explains how to use and configure RTL features in your application.\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe RTL support system consists of these key components:\\n\\n1. **I18nProvider**: Sets document direction based on language preference\\n2. **RadixDirectionProvider**: Propagates direction context to UI components\\n3. **Language Configuration**: Defines direction for each supported language\\n4. **CSS Logical Properties**: Used for direction-aware styling\\n\\n## How RTL Works\\n\\nWhen a user selects an RTL language like Arabic, several changes occur automatically:\\n\\n1. The `dir` attribute on the HTML document is set to `rtl`\\n2. Radix UI components adjust their alignment and direction\\n3. Tailwind\'s logical properties (`start`/`end` instead of `left`/`right`) respond accordingly\\n4. Text alignment and flow direction are reversed\\n\\n## Configuring Languages\\n\\nTo add RTL language support, define it in the language configuration:\\n\\n```tsx\\n// In your code that uses language configuration\\nconst languages = [\\n  {\\n    code: \'en\',\\n    name: \'English\',\\n    direction: \'ltr\',\\n    // Other properties...\\n  },\\n  {\\n    code: \'ar\',\\n    name: \'Arabic\',\\n    direction: \'rtl\',\\n    // Other properties...\\n  }\\n];\\n```\\n\\n## Switching to RTL Languages\\n\\nUse the language selector component to switch between RTL and LTR languages:\\n\\n```tsx\\n\'use client\';\\n\\nimport { useLanguage } from \'@/providers/i18n-provider\';\\n\\nfunction LanguageSwitcher() {\\n  const { languageCode, changeLanguage } = useLanguage();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => changeLanguage(\'en\')}>\\n        English (LTR)\\n      </button>\\n      <button onClick={() => changeLanguage(\'ar\')}>\\n        Arabic (RTL)\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## RTL-Aware Components\\n\\n### Using Logical Properties\\n\\nInstead of using `left` and `right`, use `start` and `end` in your styles:\\n\\n```tsx\\n// ❌ Not RTL-aware\\n<div className=\\"pl-4 ml-2 border-l\\">Content</div>\\n\\n// ✅ RTL-aware\\n<div className=\\"ps-4 ms-2 border-s\\">Content</div>\\n```\\n\\nTailwind CSS supports logical properties with these prefixes:\\n- `ps-` / `pe-` (padding-start/end)\\n- `ms-` / `me-` (margin-start/end)\\n- `start-` / `end-` (positioning)\\n- `border-s` / `border-e` (border-start/end)\\n- `rounded-s` / `rounded-e` (border-radius-start/end)\\n\\n### Checking Current Direction\\n\\nYou can check the current language direction in client components:\\n\\n```tsx\\n\'use client\';\\n\\nimport { useLanguage } from \'@/providers/i18n-provider\';\\n\\nfunction DirectionAwareComponent() {\\n  const { language } = useLanguage();\\n  const isRTL = language.direction === \'rtl\';\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {isRTL ? (\\n        <span>RTL content</span>\\n      ) : (\\n        <span>LTR content</span>\\n      )}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Common RTL Use Cases\\n\\n### Mirroring Icons and Images\\n\\nSome icons and images need to be mirrored in RTL mode:\\n\\n```tsx\\n\'use client\';\\n\\nimport { useLanguage } from \'@/providers/i18n-provider\';\\nimport { ArrowLeft, ArrowRight } from \'lucide-react\';\\n\\nfunction DirectionalButton() {\\n  const { language } = useLanguage();\\n  const isRTL = language.direction === \'rtl\';\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button>\\n      {isRTL ? <ArrowLeft /> : <ArrowRight />}\\n      Next\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Direction-Specific Styling\\n\\nFor more complex styling needs, use conditional classes based on direction:\\n\\n```tsx\\n\'use client\';\\n\\nimport { useLanguage } from \'@/providers/i18n-provider\';\\nimport { cn } from \'@/lib/utils\';\\n\\nfunction SidebarComponent() {\\n  const { language } = useLanguage();\\n  const isRTL = language.direction === \'rtl\';\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div className={cn(\\n      \\"border p-4\\",\\n      isRTL ? \\"mr-auto\\" : \\"ml-auto\\"\\n    )}>\\n      Sidebar content\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Tables and Data Display\\n\\nFor tables and data displays, use `text-start` and `text-end`:\\n\\n```tsx\\nfunction DataTable({ columns }) {\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <thead>\\n        <tr>\\n          {columns.map((column) => (\\n            <th key={column.id} className=\\"text-start\\">\\n              {column.name}\\n            </th>\\n          ))}\\n        </tr>\\n      </thead>\\n      {/* Table body */}\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}\\n```\\n\\nFor more information about RTL support in Radix UI, refer to the [Radix UI Direction Provider documentation](https://www.radix-ui.com/primitives/docs/utilities/direction-provider).","code":"var Component=(()=>{var p=Object.create;var c=Object.defineProperty;var E=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var y=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var g=Object.getPrototypeOf,u=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var m=(l,n)=>()=>(n||l((n={exports:{}}).exports,n),n.exports),F=(l,n)=>{for(var s in n)c(l,s,{get:n[s],enumerable:!0})},a=(l,n,s,i)=>{if(n&&typeof n==\\"object\\"||typeof n==\\"function\\")for(let r of y(n))!u.call(l,r)&&r!==s&&c(l,r,{get:()=>n[r],enumerable:!(i=E(n,r))||i.enumerable});return l};var x=(l,n,s)=>(s=l!=null?p(g(l)):{},a(n||!l||!l.__esModule?c(s,\\"default\\",{value:l,enumerable:!0}):s,l)),N=l=>a(c({},\\"__esModule\\",{value:!0}),l);var t=m((v,o)=>{o.exports=_jsx_runtime});var L={};F(L,{default:()=>h,frontmatter:()=>w});var e=x(t()),w={title:\\"RTL Support\\",descripti